You keep the sorted cards in your left hand.When you pick up a new card with your right hand,
you compare it with the cards in your left hand from right to left.You shift the larger cards to the right until you find the correct spot for the new card, 
then insert it.At any step , 
the array is split into two parts:Left side (0 to i-1): Already sorted
Right side ($i$ to $n-1$): Unsorted elements waiting to be inserted.


public static void insertionSort(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            int key = arr[i];
            int j = i - 1;

            // Shift elements of arr[0..i-1] that are greater than key
            // to one position ahead of their current position
            while (j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {
                arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
                j--;
            }

            // Place key at its correct sorted position
            arr[j + 1] = key;
        }
    }

Best Case Time Complexity: O(N)
Occurs when the array is already sorted. The inner condition fails on the first check, resulting in only N-1 comparisons.

Worst Case Time Complexity: O(N^2)
Occurs when the array is reverse sorted. For each element at index i, you shift i elements to the right.

Average Case Time Complexity: O(N^2)
On average, you shift half of the sorted subarray.

Auxiliary Space Complexity: O(1)
In-place sorting algorithm requiring constant extra memory.
